<title>GERMANY: Volksfuersorge 96 net profit up 6.2 pct.</title>
<headline>Volksfuersorge 96 net profit up 6.2 pct.</headline>
<dateline>HAMBURG, Germany 1997-05-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Calendar 1996</p>
<p>(in millions of marks unless otherwise stated)</p>
<p>    Net profit				   90.8    vs 85.5</p>
<p>    Of which transferred to reserves     25	vs n/a</p>
<p>    Dividend total			     66	vs 60</p>
<p>    Dividend (already reported)	    15 dmk  vs 15 dmk</p>
<p>    DVFA earnings per share		  36 dmk  vs n/a</p>
<p>Volksfuersorge Deutsche Lebensversicherung AG</p>
<p>    Net profit				   80	vs 78</p>
<p>Volksfuersorge Deutsche Sachversicherung AG</p>
<p>    Net profit				   48	vs 43.6</p>
<p>    Premium income			     1,190   vs 1,210</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name of the German insurer is Volksfuersorge Holding AG, a unit of the AMB Holding insurance group. Chief executive Hans Jaeger told a news conference that 1996 had seen good earnings power and sharply falling costs at Volksfuersorge units. But growth had continued to slow in the insurance industry. Premiums had risen only about five percent in life and health business, half of growth rates 10 years ago, while non-life business stagnated, dropping 3.7 percent in car insurance.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
